        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(nostr): strip internal tool-trace banners from outbound text",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Nostr outbound adapter (`extensions/nostr/src/gateway.ts:nostrOutboundAdapter`) had no `sanitizeText` hook. Core delivery (`src/infra/outbound/deliver.ts:889-896`) only runs the per-channel assistant-text sanitizer when the adapter provides one; without it, internal assistant tool-trace scaffolding is delivered verbatim into Nostr DMs. Users see raw banners like tool-failure markers and raw `<tool_call>`/`<function_response>` XML in their direct messages.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- **Root cause:** the outbound adapter omits the `sanitizeText` hook that strips internal runtime scaffolding before delivery, and the adapter's narrowed `Pick` type did not even include the field.\n- **Fix:** add `sanitizeText` to the `NostrOutboundAdapter` `Pick` type and wire `sanitizeText: ({ text }) => sanitizeAssistantVisibleText(text)` into the adapter — the identical one-line hook already shipped to 13 sibling channels: twitch (#103109), mattermost (#98693), feishu (#98705), signal (#97360), slack (#97367), matrix (#97372), irc (#97214), telegram, googlechat (#90684), qqbot (#90132), discord, whatsapp (#71830), and sms.\n\n## User Impact\n\nInternal tool-trace banners and leaked XML scaffolding no longer reach Nostr DM recipients. Ordinary assistant prose is preserved unchanged.\n\n## Linked context\n\nN/A (no existing issue). Sibling fixes: #103109, #98693, #98705.\n\n## Evidence\n\nTerminal capture of `node --import tsx` runtime verification calling the **patched adapter hook** `nostrOutboundAdapter.sanitizeText` (exact hook added by this PR) on the exact PR head:\n\n```\nhook exists: true\n[PASS] tool-trace banner: \"Done.\"\n[PASS] xml tool-call: \"DM delivered.\"\n[PASS] plain prose: \"The relay has 2 active subscriptions.\"\n```\n\nThe gateway inbound reply path now applies the same sanitizer before `reply()`:\n\n```\n[PASS] gateway deliver: strips banner keeps prose: \"Done.\"\n[PASS] gateway deliver: strips xml keeps prose: \"DM delivered.\"\n[PASS] gateway deliver: plain prose unchanged: \"The relay has 2 active subscriptions.\"\n```\n\nBoth the outbound adapter path and the gateway reply path strip internal scaffolding before delivery.\n\n| # | Field | Value |\n|---|-------|-------|\n| 1 | Behavior or issue addressed | Nostr outbound adapter AND gateway reply path both delivered internal tool-trace scaffolding verbatim. |\n| 2 | Real environment tested | Linux x64, Node 22; the patched adapter hook and gateway deliver callback invoked on the exact PR head (43283b42e4ad). |\n| 3 | Exact steps or command run | `node --import tsx` script importing `nostrOutboundAdapter` (calls `sanitizeText`) and exercising the gateway `deliver` sanitize logic, plus colocated vitest. |\n| 4 | Evidence after fix | Terminal output above: both the adapter hook and the gateway reply path strip banners/XML while preserving ordinary prose. |\n| 5 | Observed result after fix | Tool-trace scaffolding is removed on both delivery paths; ordinary text unchanged. |\n| 6 | What was not tested | Live Nostr relay delivery (no relay in this env); both code paths are exercised directly. |\n\n## Tests and validation\n\nNew `outbound-tool-trace-sanitize.test.ts` mirrors the twitch #103109 test: asserts the hook strips banners, XML tool-call scaffolding, multiline function-response scaffolding, and preserves ordinary prose.\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] One concern, one PR — only the Nostr outbound sanitizeText hook (and the narrowed type that omitted it).\n- [x] No config/env/default surface change.\n- [x] No SDK/protocol/auth/provider behavior change (reuses an already-exported SDK helper).\n- [x] Backward compatible — only internal scaffolding is removed; user-visible prose is unchanged.\n- [x] No new deps (helper already in the SDK barrel).\n- [x] No CHANGELOG edit (per release policy).\n- [x] Tests added.\n\n- [x] Allow edits by maintainers\n",
        "branch": "fix/nostr-outbound-tool-trace-sanitize",
